---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureIWorkToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureIWorkToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="keynote-to-pdf">
  <PdfSecureIWorkToPdf client:load kind="keynote" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Opening a Keynote deck without Keynote</h2>
      <p>
        Someone sent you a <code>.key</code> file and the presentation is in an hour. PowerPoint does
        not open it, Google Slides does not import it, and the sender is asleep in another time zone.
        The slides are already inside the file, and this Keynote to PDF page extracts them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where the PDF comes from</h2>
      <p>
        A Keynote document is a zip archive. Each time Keynote saves, it writes a full PDF rendering
        of the deck to <code>QuickLook/Preview.pdf</code>, so Finder, Quick Look and Mail can show
        the slides without the app. That preview has one page per slide, at the deck's own aspect
        ratio, with the real fonts embedded.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page unzips the archive in your browser and gives you that PDF byte for byte. Nothing is
        rendered again, so a substituted font cannot move a title off the slide. That is a common
        failure of server-side deck conversion.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What survives and what does not</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Element</th>
              <th scope="col">In the PDF</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Slide layout, text, shapes, charts</td>
              <td>Yes, as vectors. Sharp at any zoom</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Images and photographic backgrounds</td>
              <td>Yes, embedded at the resolution Keynote used</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Builds and animations</td>
              <td>Flattened to the final built state. PDF has no animation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Slide transitions</td>
              <td>Gone, for the same reason</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Presenter notes</td>
              <td>Not included. The preview is the audience view</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Videos and audio</td>
              <td>The poster frame only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hyperlinks on slides</td>
              <td>Usually preserved as PDF link annotations</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        These are the same trade-offs as Keynote's own File → Export To → PDF with default settings,
        because it is the same renderer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Slide size and printing</h2>
      <p>
        Keynote decks are usually 16:9 (1920 × 1080 points scaled) or 4:3, so the PDF pages are
        landscape and not a standard paper size. That is fine on screen. For printing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Choose <strong>Fit to page</strong> in the print dialogue. The aspect ratios do not match
          A4 or Letter, so scaling is always necessary.
        </li>
        <li>
          For handouts, use your reader's <strong>multiple pages per sheet</strong> option. Two
          16:9 slides fit well on a portrait A4.
        </li>
        <li>
          Landscape orientation wastes less paper than portrait for a 16:9 deck, but gives one slide
          per sheet.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why the conversion takes seconds</h2>
      <p>
        An upload service must receive the whole file before it can start. Decks are big
        (photographs, embedded video, high-resolution backgrounds), and 300 MB over a typical upload
        connection takes several minutes before any work starts. Then it renders and you download.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here there is no transfer. The tab reads the archive from disk and inflates one entry. The
        speed depends only on your disk, which for a 300 MB deck is a second or two.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>If there is no preview inside</h2>
      <p>
        Keynote can be set not to save previews, and very old versions did not save them. For that
        file, the options are:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Ask the sender to open Keynote → Settings → General, tick <strong>Include preview in
          document by default</strong>, save again, and send it again.
        </li>
        <li>Or ask them for File → Export To → PDF, which takes them ten seconds.</li>
        <li>
          If the file is a macOS package and not a single file, compress it to a .zip first and drop
          that here.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Next steps with the PDF</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a> gives one image per slide, to embed elsewhere.</li>
        <li><a href="/merge-pdf">Merge PDF</a> combines several decks into one handout.</li>
        <li><a href="/compress-pdf-to-2mb">Compress to 2 MB</a> makes a photo-heavy deck small enough to email.</li>
        <li>
          <a href="/password-protect-pdf">Password protect it</a> if the deck is confidential and you
          will share it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
